' Current backend direction: arch/diagrams/backend-current.puml and arch/decisions/.
@startuml BackendDataOwnership
title Backend: data ownership and exchange channels
left to right direction
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam nodesep 55

package "Client Zone" #FFE5DD {
  actor "Client (CLI / TUI)" as Client
}

package "Server Zone" #E0F4E0 {
  component "hermes-document-service\nPublic API + session middleware\nInternal authorization API\nOutbox publisher" as HDS
  component "hermes-notification-service\nPublic API + session middleware\nEvent consumer + inbox policies" as HNS

  database "Document PostgreSQL\nOWNER: document-service\n\nusers + credentials\nworkspaces + memberships\ninvitations\ndocuments + subscriptions\nemail confirmation requests\noutbox_events" as Domain
  database "Redis: sessions\nCONTRACT OWNER: document-service\n\nsession:{token_hash}\n  session_id, user_id, expires_at, auth_epoch\nuser-auth:{user_id}\n  enabled, auth_epoch\nuser-sessions:{user_id}\n  session IDs / token hashes" as Sessions
  queue "Kafka: domain-events.v1\nPRODUCER: document-service\nCONSUMER: notification-service" as Kafka
  database "Notification PostgreSQL\nOWNER: notification-service\n\nnotifications\nnotification_preferences\nprocessed_events\nquarantined_events" as Inbox
}

Client --> HDS : Public HTTP or gRPC over TLS\nlogin/logout, profiles, spaces, documents\nsession token on protected calls
Client --> HNS : Public HTTP or gRPC over TLS\ninbox + count, mark read, preferences\nsame session token; polling in v1
HDS --> Domain : Exclusive domain reads/writes\nmutation + outbox in one transaction
HDS --> Sessions : Create / validate / revoke sessions\nmanage user authentication state
HNS --> Sessions : Read-only session validation\nno HDS call for authentication
HDS --> Kafka : Publish committed outbox facts\noriginal event_id retained on retries
Kafka --> HNS : At-least-once event consumption
HNS --> Inbox : Exclusive reads/writes\nnotification + processing receipt transaction
HNS --> HDS : Internal authenticated service API\nbatch current eligibility checks\nnot session validation

note bottom of Sessions
  Redis is the authoritative store for active sessions, not a PostgreSQL cache.
  Token hashes only; session TTL is bounded by expires_at.
  HNS validates session + enabled user + matching auth_epoch atomically.
  No workspace roles, emails or document bodies in session records.
  User session index supports cleanup; epoch change revokes all sessions.
  Redis unavailable: protected requests fail as temporarily unavailable.
  Missing session/auth state: require login; never recreate it from an event.
  Persistence/HA and failover consistency need an operational decision:
  restoring stale Redis state must not resurrect revoked sessions.
end note
note bottom of Kafka
  Domain events contain IDs, versions and relevant changed fields.
  They carry no bearer tokens, credentials, private emails or document bodies.
  UserCreated/UserDeleted do not directly create user inbox notifications.
  Kafka delivery is not the authority for active sessions or current access.
end note
note bottom of Domain
  Ownership means one service defines schema and allowed mutations.
  Stores may share a PostgreSQL instance, but HNS has no direct domain access.
  Clients access service APIs only, never databases, Redis or Kafka.
end note
note bottom of HNS
  Proposal: current access checks use the HDS internal API in v1.
  There is no local authorization projection yet.
  Future WebSocket delivery follows durable notification persistence.
end note
@enduml

@startuml BackendLogicalData
title Backend: logical entities and ownership (selected fields)
hide circle
skinparam linetype ortho

package "document-service owns: PostgreSQL" {
  entity "users" as Users {
    * id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    username / email / display_name
    status / version
  }
  entity "user_credentials" as Credentials {
    * user_id : UUID <<FK>>
    --
    method / verifier_data / credential_version
  }
  entity "workspaces" as Workspaces {
    * id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    owner_id : UUID <<FK>>
    kind / name / version / deleted_at
  }
  entity "workspace_members" as Members {
    * workspace_id : UUID <<PK, FK>>
    * user_id : UUID <<PK, FK>>
    --
    role : owner | admin | editor | viewer
    joined_at
  }
  entity "workspace_invitations" as Invitations {
    * id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    workspace_id / inviter_id / invitee_id
    role / status / expires_at
  }
  entity "documents" as Documents {
    * id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    workspace_id : UUID <<FK>>
    title / content / version / deleted_at
    created_by / updated_by
  }
  entity "document_subscriptions" as Subscriptions {
    * document_id : UUID <<PK, FK>>
    * user_id : UUID <<PK, FK>>
  }
  entity "outbox_events" as Outbox {
    * event_id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    type / schema_version
    aggregate_type / aggregate_id / aggregate_version
    actor_id / workspace_id / occurred_at
    correlation_id / payload / publication_state
  }
  entity "email_change_requests" as EmailRequests {
    * id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    user_id / proposed_email / confirmation_token_hash
    expires_at / consumed_at
  }
}
package "document-service owns contract: Redis" {
  entity "session" as Session {
    * token_hash : key
    --
    session_id / user_id / expires_at / auth_epoch
  }
  entity "user authentication state" as AuthState {
    * user_id : key
    --
    enabled / auth_epoch
  }
}
package "notification-service owns: PostgreSQL" {
  entity "notifications" as Notifications {
    * id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    recipient_id / source_event_id <<UNIQUE pair>>
    category / type / actor_id
    workspace_id / target_type / target_id
    created_at / read_at
  }
  entity "notification_preferences" as Preferences {
    * user_id : UUID <<PK>>
    * category : users | workspaces | documents <<PK>>
    --
    enabled : boolean
  }
  entity "processed_events" as Receipts {
    * consumer_name <<PK>>
    * event_id : UUID <<PK>>
    --
    processed_at
  }
}

Users ||--o{ Credentials
Users ||--o{ Workspaces : owns
Users ||--o{ Members
Workspaces ||--o{ Members
Workspaces ||--o{ Invitations
Workspaces ||--o{ Documents
Documents ||--o{ Subscriptions
Users ||--o{ Subscriptions
Users ||--o{ EmailRequests
Session ..> Users : user_id reference
AuthState ..> Users : user_id reference
Notifications ..> Users : recipient_id reference
Notifications ..> Documents : optional target reference
Preferences ..> Users : user_id reference
Notifications ..> Outbox : source_event_id reference

note bottom of Members
  Authorization is a relationship, not a single user.role attribute.
  One user may be admin in workspace A and viewer in workspace B.
  Document permissions derive from current membership + workspace/document state.
  owner_id and owner membership must remain consistent in one transaction.
  Subscription selects notification interest; it never grants access.
end note
note bottom of Notifications
  Dashed cross-store references are logical IDs, not SQL foreign keys.
  Notifications do not own profiles, documents or access permissions.
  A notification does not grant access to its target.
  Minimal removal/deletion notices use separate visibility policies.
end note
@enduml

@startuml BackendReadChannels
title Backend: authentication, authorization and event-derived inbox
autonumber
actor Client
participant "document-service" as HDS
database "Document PostgreSQL" as Domain
database "Redis sessions" as Redis
queue Kafka
participant "notification-service" as HNS
database "Notification PostgreSQL" as Inbox

== Login ==
Client -> HDS : Authenticate(credentials)
HDS -> Domain : Verify active account and credentials
HDS -> Redis : Create session against current enabled auth epoch
Redis --> HDS : Persisted
HDS --> Client : Opaque token + expires_at
note over HDS, Redis
  Login and disabling an account must serialize authentication state changes.
  Creating a session must not re-enable a disabled user.
end note

== Read a document ==
Client -> HDS : GetDocument(id, token)
HDS -> Redis : Validate hash, expiry, enabled state and epoch
Redis --> HDS : Authenticated user_id or invalid
HDS -> Domain : Read document with current membership/access predicate
HDS --> Client : Document or access error

== Read inbox ==
Client -> HNS : ListInboxWithCount(filters, cursor, token)
HNS -> Redis : Same session validation; no call to HDS
Redis --> HNS : Authenticated user_id or invalid
HNS -> Inbox : Read recipient's candidates
HNS -> HDS : Internal batch eligibility(user_id, target IDs, notice types)
HDS -> Domain : Current account, memberships and target state
HDS --> HNS : Eligibility decisions only
HNS -> Inbox : Apply visibility to page + count + mark-all boundary
HNS --> Client : Visible notifications, count, cursor, boundary
note over HNS, Inbox
  Logical flow, not a final pagination/query implementation.
  Filtering must cover the full count and fill pages correctly;
  filtering one raw page and using an unfiltered count is insufficient.
  Batch checks avoid one HDS request per notification.
  Unavailable eligibility checks return unavailable, not an unfiltered inbox.
end note
Client -> HDS : Open notification target through its owning service API
HDS -> Redis : Validate session
HDS -> Domain : Recheck current access and read object
HDS --> Client : Object or unavailable

== Domain mutation to durable inbox ==
Client -> HDS : Update object(expected_version, token)
HDS -> Redis : Validate session
HDS -> Domain : Transaction: current authorization + mutation + outbox
HDS --> Client : Result after commit
HDS -> Kafka : Outbox publisher: committed event, retries allowed
Kafka -> HNS : Event with stable event_id
HNS -> HDS : Batch eligibility if event policy needs current domain state
HNS -> Inbox : Transaction: preferences + notifications + processed receipt
HNS -> Kafka : Commit offset after durable processing

== Account deletion across two stores ==
note over HDS, Redis
  PostgreSQL and Redis do not share a transaction.
  Proposal: after identity confirmation, first disable user auth state
  and advance epoch in Redis; then perform the checked domain deletion
  and outbox transaction in PostgreSQL. Deletion success requires both.
  If the domain transaction fails, keep the account safely signed out;
  a reconciler restores login only after proving the account remains active.
  Operation IDs/reconciliation and races with in-flight commands need
  a dedicated deletion protocol; Kafka alone cannot revoke sessions promptly.
end note
note over HDS, HNS
  Sessions are stored in Redis, not PostgreSQL (ADR-001).
  HNS validates sessions directly, without internal ValidateSession calls.
  Current authorization API is a proposal, not a settled requirement.
  Replacing it with a Kafka-fed projection introduces an access-revocation
  delay and needs an explicit consistency contract before adoption.
end note
@enduml
